WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.200
- [Kelly] Have a seat.

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.490
- Thank you.

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:05.101
- I mean, I'm really
into your vibe right now.

00:00:05.101 --> 00:00:06.120
- Oh, thank you. Thank you.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.430
- Yes. It's nice to see you again.

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:10.080
- Nice to see you.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.820
- Last time I saw you,
you didn't have a kid.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:12.653
You have a two year old, now.

00:00:12.653 --> 00:00:14.010
- And you didn't have an Emmy.

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:15.630
- I, well, boom.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:16.463
Look at us.

00:00:16.463 --> 00:00:17.296
- Emmy and a baby.

00:00:17.296 --> 00:00:18.300
I mean that's not doing too bad.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.363
- We're killing it.

00:00:19.363 --> 00:00:20.850
How is it? Two, right?

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:21.683
Two years old?

00:00:21.683 --> 00:00:22.516
- She's two years old.

00:00:22.516 --> 00:00:25.173
I was pretty much pregnant
during the whole pandemic.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:27.600
Great way to keep it a secret,

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.580
because all your meetings
are from here up, you know?

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:30.750
- [Kelly] Oh my gosh, that's awesome.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:35.750
- Yeah, and I had a
daughter in August of 2020.

00:00:35.934 --> 00:00:36.767
- [Kelly] Wow.

00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:37.600
- Yeah.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:38.433
- That's so cool.

00:00:38.433 --> 00:00:39.266
- Yeah, it was, it's the best.

00:00:39.266 --> 00:00:40.099
- I'm very happy for you.

00:00:40.099 --> 00:00:41.100
I have kiddos and they're
six and eight now,

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.510
but that's magical.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:45.254
Every step is challenging but
every step is magical as well.

00:00:45.254 --> 00:00:48.570
But being a first time
parent can be hard, though.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.940
So who's your go-to for advice?

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.580
- I mean, I have a
couple little mom groups.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:54.413
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:54.413 --> 00:00:56.580
- Mom friends. And then
my own mom, of course.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.890
- Yeah. That helps out as well.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:00.644
You went through a challenging
time with your mama, right?

00:01:00.644 --> 00:01:01.477
- Yeah.

00:01:01.477 --> 00:01:02.430
- I mean, I feel like, wait.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.080
I could have asked any
of us that question.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:04.913
- I know, right?

00:01:04.913 --> 00:01:06.930
- I feel like we all, even are
mothers with their mothers.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:07.763
Yeah.

00:01:07.763 --> 00:01:09.210
- The relationship is such a journey

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:10.590
and that's what's beautiful about it.

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:13.410
But, you know, it's one of
the things I write about

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:16.800
in my book that I was really
ashamed of and embarrassed

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.357
for a long time, is that I was like,

00:01:19.357 --> 00:01:22.140
estranged from my mother
for like five, six years.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:23.000
Didn't speak to her.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:23.833
- Wow.

00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:26.460
- Not even an email. And that's on me.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:29.370
And the beautiful thing
about becoming pregnant

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:32.190
was it sort of brought us together again

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:33.720
and we've reconciled since then.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:34.553
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:34.553 --> 00:01:35.386
- And, you know,

00:01:35.386 --> 00:01:37.230
continue building a
wonderful relationship.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.660
- Yeah, it gives you a fresh
perspective on parenting, too.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.283
- Oh, a hundred percent.
'Cause my mom had four.

00:01:42.283 --> 00:01:45.240
And I only have one and
I'm like, how do you?

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:46.410
- Yeah, my mom had three

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:47.970
and I had two and I'm just like, whoa.

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:48.803
- [Constance] I know.

00:01:48.803 --> 00:01:49.636
- You're outnumbered.

00:01:49.636 --> 00:01:51.150
I mean, I'm outnumbered, I'm a single mom.

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:53.224
But like, but it's a lot,

00:01:53.224 --> 00:01:54.057
- It is a lot.

00:01:54.057 --> 00:01:54.890
- To have a lot of kids running around

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:56.250
and make sure you give
attention to each one equally.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:57.180
That's like, hard.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:58.013
- Yeah.

00:01:58.013 --> 00:01:59.100
- But wait, did you have
a lot of rules growing up?

00:01:59.100 --> 00:01:59.933
Was it strict?

00:01:59.933 --> 00:02:01.110
My mom was a very strict mom.

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:02.697
She had to be, She was a
single parent for a lot of it.

00:02:02.697 --> 00:02:05.100
- No, my parents were not strict at all.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:05.933
- [Kelly] What?

00:02:05.933 --> 00:02:06.810
- In fact, what now
that I have a daughter,

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:09.000
it's interesting when I
think about how I grew up.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.430
How like, after school,

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:14.184
we would just leave the house
and roam the neighborhood.

00:02:14.184 --> 00:02:16.350
My mom had no idea where we were.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:17.888
We'd go to the woods.

00:02:17.888 --> 00:02:18.757
- [Kelly] Oh that was me, too.

00:02:18.757 --> 00:02:20.700
- Yeah. But like, it wasn't a big deal.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:22.070
- You had to be home by at
least the sun going down.

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:23.700
- As long as you were home by dinner.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:24.721
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:24.721 --> 00:02:25.554
- And we always were.

00:02:25.554 --> 00:02:28.080
But then we would also go
to random strangers houses.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.300
Me and my little sister,
we'd knock in their doors,

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.270
because we weren't allowed to
have a dog, but we love dogs.

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:34.890
So we would knock on strangers' doors,

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.780
and say, "Hi, do you have a dog?

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.730
Can we walk your dog?"

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:40.104
And usually they said yes.

00:02:40.104 --> 00:02:41.370
So, yeah.

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:43.320
- Just let strangers, this
is such a different time.

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:45.214
- I mean we were like
eight and 10 years old

00:02:45.214 --> 00:02:47.214
so like, it doesn't seem like.

00:02:47.214 --> 00:02:49.292
- Could have been a dateline
special in the making though.

00:02:49.292 --> 00:02:52.830
Just randomly walking
up to stranger's houses.

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:54.780
It's so funny what I was
able to get away with

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:56.750
as a kid and I would never let my kid do.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.440
- Oh yeah, my dad let me walk,

00:02:58.440 --> 00:02:59.910
he worked at a college

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:02.400
and sometimes we'd go to
work with him in the summers.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:03.233
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:03.233 --> 00:03:04.560
- And he'd just let us walk
around the college campus

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.570
by ourselves at like eight.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:07.740
- [Kelly] Wow.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:08.573
- Yeah. We were fine.

00:03:08.573 --> 00:03:09.930
- Maybe though, it all depends
on which kid though too.

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:11.010
Like I would maybe,

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:13.890
I would definitely let my
daughter do that, but not my son.

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:17.700
- Well and also this was
me and my younger sister

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.320
and I have two older sisters

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:22.620
and I always think maybe that
they were stricter with them.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:23.453
- [Kelly] Oh definitely, yeah.

00:03:23.453 --> 00:03:25.170
- But by the time you get to
the third you're like whatever

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:26.730
- [Kelly] They're fine.

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:29.843
We're back with Constance
Wu, You all give it up.

00:03:30.718 --> 00:03:32.107
That was a little bit of her new movie

00:03:32.107 --> 00:03:34.110
"Lyle, Lyle Crocodile".

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:36.660
So your new movie is based
on the children's book,

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:37.860
is your daughter a big reader?

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:38.940
She into it?

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:39.773
- Oh yes.

00:03:39.773 --> 00:03:41.670
I mean she can't read
yet because she's two,

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:43.740
but she is obsessed with books.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:47.730
I have to read her maybe
10, 20 books every night.

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:48.563
- A day?

00:03:48.563 --> 00:03:51.360
- Well a total a day, but
at least 10 at night, yeah.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:52.356
- Oh my god.

00:03:52.356 --> 00:03:55.782
I'm like two, and I like just one.

00:03:55.782 --> 00:03:56.615
- [Constance] Yeah.

00:03:56.615 --> 00:03:57.448
- Because it takes a
while to read through it.

00:03:57.448 --> 00:03:58.281
Do you do voices?

00:03:58.281 --> 00:03:59.190
I sometimes do voices.

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:00.660
- I actually don't do voices.

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:01.801
Because the goal is to

00:04:01.801 --> 00:04:02.634
- You're an actor.

00:04:02.634 --> 00:04:04.181
- Well the goal is to
get them to fall asleep.

00:04:04.181 --> 00:04:05.370
So I find

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:06.203
- Oh I'm an idiot.

00:04:06.203 --> 00:04:08.880
- Soothing, monotone voice.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:09.713
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:09.713 --> 00:04:11.670
- If you just do it like
this, she starts nodding.

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:12.630
- [Kelly] Like a Calm App.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:14.910
- But maybe it's not working
if I'm reading 10 books

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:17.340
- I know, maybe a new approach.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:18.300
- You know.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:19.500
- [Kelly] But your new book,

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.060
so it's called "Making a Scene".

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.560
So why did you name it that?

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:25.320
- Well it's sort of has two meanings.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.510
One is about how art is really healing.

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:29.490
And for me that has been acting.

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:31.380
And in acting you make scenes

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:33.600
whether it's in a play or like a movie.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.690
And the other is sort of the symbolic idea

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:39.510
of what it means when
somebody makes a scene.

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:41.580
And growing up in the south

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.350
it was wasn't considered ladylike.

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:44.400
- Girl, I know.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:45.233
- Yeah.

00:04:45.233 --> 00:04:46.066
- I grew up there.

00:04:46.066 --> 00:04:47.220
- Yeah, but you know, the thing is

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:49.291
if you're an emotional person, like I was

00:04:49.291 --> 00:04:50.124
(Kelly blows air)

00:04:50.124 --> 00:04:52.080
Yeah, repressed feelings don't go away

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:53.880
just because you will them to.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:55.890
They're inevitably gonna come out.

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:56.723
- Yeah.

00:04:56.723 --> 00:04:57.556
- In another way.

00:04:57.556 --> 00:05:00.270
And you know, I think there
was like this big fiasco

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:03.330
on Twitter because I said
some very negative things

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:06.330
about a show that I truly loved being on.

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:08.130
But it was within the context

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:10.830
of being sexually harassed on that show.

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:13.620
Being intimidated, being controlled

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:16.860
and me wanting to swallow
that and hide that.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:19.110
So I didn't stay in the reputation

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:21.690
of the only Asian American network sitcom.

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:24.480
- Do you feel obligated to say something?

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:25.530
Do you react ever?

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:26.940
Like, you know what I'm saying?

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:29.670
- Well, I mean there was
definitely a social media pile on

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:32.040
which I understood because
if you don't know me

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.230
it just looks like "Oh she
had this huge hit movie

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:37.110
and now she thinks she's,
you know, outgrown the show".

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:37.943
Which wasn't.

00:05:37.943 --> 00:05:39.630
Yeah, but you led with "You don't know me"

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:40.860
which is "They don't know you" though.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:43.320
- Yeah, but it's easy to
think you know a public figure

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:44.153
based on

00:05:44.153 --> 00:05:44.986
- Yeah.

00:05:44.986 --> 00:05:47.100
- The limited information
that you have of them.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:50.310
It's easy to construct that narrative.

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:53.790
But you know, I had been a
public figure for a while then

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:56.940
so I thought I was pretty
strong and pretty capable.

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:58.500
But it was actually a few DMs

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.960
from a former colleague of mine,

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:02.910
an Asian actress who you know,

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:05.948
you think should be on your side.

00:06:05.948 --> 00:06:09.870
That sort of convinced me
that my life was worthless.

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:12.240
And you know, I ended up in the hospital

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:15.200
because I tried to take my own
life, which was really scary.

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:16.620
- Yeah.

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:18.690
- But ultimately it was a good thing

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:21.540
because it made me seek help.

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:22.472
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:22.472 --> 00:06:23.400
- And I wouldn't have
been able to talk about it

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:24.233
three years ago

00:06:24.233 --> 00:06:27.270
because I was still very
like raw and wounded.

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:28.192
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:28.192 --> 00:06:29.100
- But I think the thing I've learned now

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:31.840
is that through a lot of therapy and like

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:35.673
looking at what my values truly are,

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:38.970
I've developed the tools to handle it.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:39.971
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:39.971 --> 00:06:42.210
And so I wanna talk about
it so other young women

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:44.100
who might be struggling
with the same thing

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:45.120
know they're not alone

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:47.280
and that there is a light
at the end of the tunnel.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:48.420
It takes work

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:49.800
- But you can get there.
- [Kelly] Work.

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:50.811
- Yeah.

00:06:50.811 --> 00:06:51.644
- I literally just said this

00:06:51.644 --> 00:06:52.740
because people are like,
"Oh, how did you get through

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:54.270
like certain things or
whatever in my life"

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:55.778
and I was like, "Oh, it's a lot of work".

00:06:55.778 --> 00:06:56.611
- [Constance] It's work.

00:06:56.611 --> 00:06:58.740
- And it's honestly like
people frown sometimes upon

00:06:58.740 --> 00:06:59.970
especially in the south, where we're from

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:03.540
like at therapy and it's like
these are trained people.

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:04.373
- [Constance] Yeah.

00:07:04.373 --> 00:07:06.930
- That have tools to help
me get through something.

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:08.340
Why would I not want that?

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:10.440
Like why, why would I turn that away?

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:12.330
And by the way, I'm so sorry too

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:14.070
because it's hard in the moment

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:15.630
when you're already
feeling down about yourself

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:18.180
and doubting yourself and
what you did or your actions.

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:20.370
But for somebody else to come in

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:22.830
and invade that and
project their shortcomings

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:23.760
because that's what that is.

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:24.672
I don't even know who
you're talking about,

00:07:24.672 --> 00:07:25.505
and I don't know what
you're talking about.

00:07:25.505 --> 00:07:27.570
- It's actually someone
you think is an ally.

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:28.500
- But, exactly.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:30.870
And I'm like, that's their shortcomings.

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:31.810
- Of course.

00:07:31.810 --> 00:07:33.300
- That's on them.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:35.370
And it's hard to see that in the moment.

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:36.203
- It is.

00:07:36.203 --> 00:07:37.036
- And I realize that,
that it's hard for me too.

00:07:37.036 --> 00:07:40.050
But like I'm really
happy that you got help

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:41.497
and I'm really happy you got the tools.

00:07:41.497 --> 00:07:42.330
- Yeah, Thank you.

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:45.390
- I did too, to get through
my, I think we all do.

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:47.240
We all have stuff that we go through.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:49.290
And thank you for sharing that.

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:50.378
I didn't know all of that.

00:07:50.378 --> 00:07:51.211
- Does anyone have a Kleenex?

00:07:51.211 --> 00:07:52.044
- Oh, I do.

00:07:52.044 --> 00:07:53.807
Girl, I cry all the time on here.

00:07:53.807 --> 00:07:55.567
- I can't and you know,
I don't wanna mess it up.

00:07:55.567 --> 00:07:56.550
- No, I cry all the time.

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:57.690
- They did such a beautiful job.

00:07:57.690 --> 00:07:58.680
- I always feel sorry for Glow.

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:00.267
I'm like "I apologize".

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:02.760
So how do you feel though

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:04.797
representation has changed
for Asian Americans?

00:08:04.797 --> 00:08:07.020
I feel like it's gotten better

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:08.430
but I feel like there's still work to do.

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:10.950
- Well you know, I think
it has gotten better

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:12.510
in that it's more there.

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:13.980
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:16.110
- I still think the
conversation is too focused

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:18.360
on positive representation
of Asian Americans.

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:19.920
And I think that's another trap.

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:21.900
I don't think we need
positive representation.

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:25.200
I think we need whole human representation

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:27.660
which includes our faults,

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:31.230
which includes talking about
things that are difficult

00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:34.350
in the community such as
the internalized misogyny

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:36.570
that happens such as me
talking about what happened

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:40.893
on the show, holding each
other accountable out of love.

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:46.800
You know, because to just
have an Asian love interest

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:48.570
with six pack abs.

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:51.600
Why can't we have one that
doesn't have six pack abs?

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:52.433
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:08:52.433 --> 00:08:53.266
- Do you know what I mean?

00:08:53.266 --> 00:08:54.435
- Totally got some under here.

00:08:54.435 --> 00:08:55.268
(Constance laughs)

00:08:55.268 --> 00:08:56.502
I'm just doing a load.

00:08:56.502 --> 00:08:57.335
Of laundry.

00:08:57.335 --> 00:09:00.630
- The point should be that
you are loved and worthy

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:01.710
as you are.

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:02.543
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:09:02.543 --> 00:09:06.720
- With all your flaws, even
if you don't have a six pack.

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:09.240
- I think, I mean I'm pretty average.

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:10.740
I feel like I'm the
most average human ever

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:13.110
and I like a real representation.

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:13.965
- Yeah.

00:09:13.965 --> 00:09:14.881
- [Kelly] Like, you know what I'm saying?

00:09:14.881 --> 00:09:15.714
- Same.

00:09:15.714 --> 00:09:16.547
- Like when you're seeing a whole thing,

00:09:16.547 --> 00:09:19.500
I want the real, I want all of
it because I have all of it.

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:22.140
- But Asian Americans have
a history of being mocked

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:24.660
in especially in popular culture.

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:25.493
- Yeah.

00:09:25.493 --> 00:09:28.500
- And I think that is
why there has been this

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:31.623
sort of overcorrection
of wanting to be cool.

00:09:31.623 --> 00:09:34.140
Such as like when I did
"Fresh Off the Boat",

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:37.380
people were very mad that
my character had an accent.

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:40.050
So much so that some actors would say,

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:44.700
Asian actors would say, "I
refuse to play any character

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:46.020
that has an Asian accent".

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:49.997
And I almost feel like
that refusal almost.

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:52.290
- You're saying there's
something wrong with it.

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:56.970
- Yeah, almost strengthens
the antiquated idea

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:58.830
that there's something inherently shameful

00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:00.420
or buffoonish about an accent.

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:01.253
- [Kelly] I love accents.

00:10:01.253 --> 00:10:03.540
- When you look at it objectively

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:06.270
all it means is that
you know two languages.

00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:08.040
That is frigging cool.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:09.252
- [Kelly] That's something to be proud of.

00:10:09.252 --> 00:10:10.085
- Yes.

00:10:10.085 --> 00:10:10.918
- I've been trying for years.

00:10:10.918 --> 00:10:12.570
Yeah, so let's make our choice.

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:13.830
Yeah, yours is beautiful.

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:16.170
But let's make our choices

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:19.830
rather than making our
choices a reaction to a system

00:10:19.830 --> 00:10:22.710
that has mocked us and
doesn't understand us.

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:24.360
Let's be creative, not reactive.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:25.740
Let's make our own system.

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:27.000
And if somebody doesn't get it,

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:28.590
if somebody laughs at the wrong thing,

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:29.618
- Somebody's always gonna get it.

00:10:29.618 --> 00:10:30.451
- That's always gonna get it.

00:10:30.451 --> 00:10:31.284
- Yeah, somebody, yeah.

00:10:31.284 --> 00:10:34.290
- You don't create art to
cater to ignorant people.

00:10:34.290 --> 00:10:35.123
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:10:35.123 --> 00:10:36.630
You create art because
it's out of your soul.

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:37.463
- Yeah.

00:10:37.463 --> 00:10:40.060
- So we need the six packs,
but we also need the kegs.

00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:40.893
- Yeah.

00:10:40.893 --> 00:10:43.476
(Kelly laughs)

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:45.813
I love you.

